our panelist Catherine Marshall who has  had I think more than four decades of  experience with humanitarian work we're  all much we all started we were just  children two or three years old but she  is a senior fellow at Georgetown  University's Berkeley center for  religion and world affairs and a  Georgetown visiting professor she's also  executive director of the world faiths  development dialogue which I understand  you were engaged in perhaps if not  launching definitely you're there at the  beginning but before that she had a  career with the World Bank and there she  worked for many years and then led the  World Bank's faith in ethics work  through the first decade of this century  and her books include development in  faith where mind heart and soul worked  together and I loved the fact that you  also in your article in reflections  Catherine talked about the bottom line  questions why should we care what can we  do about it what is our responsibility  and you gave us reasons to care not only  the reasons based on faith and on  compassion but also the practical  reasons for why we need to care and act  in our own self-interest on behalf of  the poor and then you talked about the  complexities and it is not simple so I  don't know how much of that you are  going to share with us again but I  commend all of you to read Katherine's  article we're happy to have you here  thank you well senator Coons has said  almost everything that I would like to  say but there there are a few points  that I think we should highlight the  first that i was struck when my son who  I think was 10 at the time challenged me  when I was speaking and saying that we  can achieve a world without poverty we  have the resources we have the know-how  we have the examples and he said you  don't really believe that do you but I  believe it with all my heart that we can  and because we can  we have the obligation to do something  about it it's a very different situation  from most of world history where poverty  was the norm but in country after  country we've seen transformations in in  a lifetime in a career so I think that  first point is one that people don't  believe that like my 10 year old son at  the time they don't fully accept that  this is something it's a transformation  that is that is our gift but is also our  obligation and our responsibility the  second thought and you you cautioned us  against using statistics and I will want  to pick up simply on two of the numbers  not the billions that we quote all the  time but the 20,000 children who die  every day and one other number that I  never can get away from because of  personal experience which is that a  woman is two thousand times more likely  to die in nazare in childbirth than she  is in Scandinavia and actually it's  rather sad that that number is not an  american number because scandinavia does  a lot better than the United States but  apart from the raw injustice of these  deaths I was scanning the newspapers  coming back from a trip there's no  mention of these deaths the women who  died the children who die they die the  people who died of AIDS die die hiding  almost in shame and yet one of the joys  of our society is that we more in every  death we we value when we treasure every  person and somehow that sense the it's  one of the things that's most  frustrating for people who work in  development is how how absent how  invisible these problems seem to be in  everyday life so I think that's one of  the challenges for all of us apart from  believing that it's possible to find  ways to communicate both the hope and  the injustice the raw in Jaws  this reading about what's happening in  North Africa and talking to people there  are I think some some important messages  for us first we live in a different  world and it's an interconnected world  that's a world that's connected not only  by cell phones and computers it's  connected by ideas and by visions and  the hopes that those young people have  are so similar to the hopes of young  people at our universities they are the  hopes for a job for respect for decency  they are but they are also impatient as  our young people are impatient and I  think that that's something we should  never forget but one thing that this  surprise revolution calls us to I think  as much as anything is a much more  intelligent and professional analysis of  what religion means in this because  religion is absolutely a vital part we  should never think that because it's not  completely visible that it's not a  fundamental part of these societies that  are profoundly religious so I think that  understanding we've been caught off  guard and not understanding the  religious nature and we need a  professional a compassionate and a sober  understanding of where religion fits my  last comment I'm going to tell you just  about one of the thousands of people  that I've worked with over the years  ayesha at jenna is a remarkable  courageous Moroccan woman she won the  Opus prize which is given for people to  buy a Minnesota foundation that's  looking for the next Mother Teresa but  she has worked for 40 years with the  people who are at the very bottom of  Morocco's society and of society across  many parts of the world including Africa  who are young unmarried mothers and she  has for 40 years battled to change the  laws that  have discriminated against women to put  women's issues into the light and to  help one by one these girls but one of  her comments echoes a little bit what I  was saying about the challenges that we  face talking to her she talks time and  time again about the knife edge we're on  the knife edge we're between hope and  great danger and if we do not act if we  act wrong if we make mistakes if we're  clumsy she's talking about herself if i  push too hard in the wrong place or if i  miss a chance we're we're facing  exciting possibilities but also a very  dangerous world a very interconnected  world and we need to keep the courageous  fighters the women and the children  always at the center of our minds quite  a challenge thank you very much  Catherine
 

